WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.513
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:15.135
Unit five: Health News. Today you will report what experts say with reported speech.

4
00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:18.099
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:22.324
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:24.208
Outbreak.

7
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:28.412
Outbreak means a sudden increase of a disease in one place.

8
00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:31.539
The outbreak was controlled within two weeks.

9
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.779
Your turn. Say: Outbreak.

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:38.542
Outbreak.

11
00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:40.401
Vaccine.

12
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:44.740
Vaccine means a medicine that protects people from a disease.

13
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.988
The vaccine protects children from measles.

14
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:50.899
Your turn. Say: Vaccine.

15
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:54.839
Vaccine.

16
00:00:55.583 --> 00:00:56.770
Public health.

17
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:00.642
Public health means the health of the whole community.

18
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.447
Public health depends on clean water.

19
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.668
Your turn. Say: Public health.

20
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:10.709
Public health.

21
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:12.881
Research study.

22
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:17.485
Research study means a careful investigation to find out facts.

23
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:20.570
A research study tested the new treatment.

24
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:23.940
Your turn. Say: Research study.

25
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.277
Research study.

26
00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:29.728
Treatment.

27
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:33.517
Treatment means medical care given to a sick person.

28
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:35.814
The treatment helped her recover.

29
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:38.732
Your turn. Say: Treatment.

30
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:42.452
Treatment.

31
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:44.195
Hospital.

32
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:48.541
Hospital means a place where sick people receive medical care.

33
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.649
The hospital opened a new children's ward.

34
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.682
Your turn. Say: Hospital.

35
00:01:57.533 --> 00:01:58.488
Hospital.

36
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.634
Awareness campaign.

37
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:06.239
Awareness campaign means an effort to make people understand a health issue.

38
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.558
The awareness campaign explained hand washing.

39
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.452
Your turn. Say: Awareness campaign.

40
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.893
Awareness campaign.

41
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:19.674
Guideline.

42
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:24.123
Guideline means official advice about the right way to do something.

43
00:02:24.717 --> 00:02:27.285
The ministry published new food guidelines.

44
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:30.280
Your turn. Say: Guideline.

45
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:34.134
Guideline.

46
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:38.596
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.587
Visal, an article says 'Doctors said the vaccine was safe.' Why was 'is' changed to 'was'?

48
00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:55.232
In reported speech we usually move the tense back. The doctor's words were 'The vaccine is safe', but the reporter writes 'said it was safe'.

49
00:02:56.317 --> 00:02:58.537
Which verbs can I use besides 'said'?

50
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:08.908
Use 'explained', 'warned' or 'announced'. Health reporters also write 'according to' a research study, which names the source.

51
00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:11.889
Why does the source matter so much?

52
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:20.732
Because health news affects people's choices. A named expert or official guideline makes a claim more trustworthy.

53
00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:23.530
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:27.764
Grammar focus: Reported speech in health news.

55
00:03:28.064 --> 00:03:29.321
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.017 --> 00:03:32.156
Doctors said that the vaccine was safe.

57
00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.526
The minister warned that the outbreak was spreading.

58
00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:39.078
The researchers explained that the treatment had worked.

59
00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:49.292
In reported speech the tense usually moves back: is → was, has worked → had worked. Always name the source: doctors said, according to the study.

60
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.933
Be careful! Don't say: He said that the vaccine is safe.

61
00:03:55.867 --> 00:03:58.551
Say: He said that the vaccine was safe.

62
00:03:58.851 --> 00:04:01.280
Move the tense back in reported speech.

63
00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:04.905
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.409
Health News You Can Trust.

65
00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:11.775
Health news can change how people live, so reporters must be careful.

66
00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:17.808
When an outbreak begins, the ministry warned that people should follow official guidelines.

67
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.226
A new research study explained that the vaccine had protected thousands of children.

68
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:28.427
Hospitals and awareness campaigns then share the advice with every community.

69
00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:32.756
Reliable treatment information always names its source.

70
00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:37.880
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:38.717 --> 00:04:41.041
Doctors said that the vaccine blank safe.

72
00:04:41.341 --> 00:04:44.368
A, was. B, is being. C, were.

73
00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:49.747
The answer is A: was.

74
00:04:50.883 --> 00:04:54.253
Official advice about the right way to do something is a blank.

75
00:04:54.553 --> 00:04:58.787
A, guideline. B, treatment. C, outbreak.

76
00:05:02.333 --> 00:05:04.623
The answer is A: guideline.

77
00:05:05.783 --> 00:05:07.957
A sudden increase of a disease is an blank.

78
00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:12.584
A, awareness campaign. B, outbreak. C, hospital.

79
00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:18.574
The answer is B: outbreak.

80
00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:21.867
Choose the correct reported sentence.

81
00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:29.153
A, She said that the study was finished.. B, She said that the study is finish.. C, She said that the study were finished..

82
00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.686
The answer is A: She said that the study was finished..

83
00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.800
According to the reading, reliable treatment information names its…

84
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.870
A, price. B, source. C, hospital.

85
00:05:48.433 --> 00:05:50.456
The answer is B: source.

86
00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:54.760
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:55.617 --> 00:05:59.276
Why is it important to get health information from a reliable source?

88
00:06:08.083 --> 00:06:09.711
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:10.011 --> 00:06:33.996
It is important because wrong health information can harm people. A reliable source, such as a doctor or a ministry guideline, is based on research and tested facts. For example, a false rumour about a vaccine may stop families from protecting their children. Therefore, I always check who wrote the information and where it came from.

90
00:06:34.296 --> 00:06:35.855
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:41.411
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reported speech in health news.

92
00:06:41.711 --> 00:06:46.708
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:06:47.008 --> 00:06:49.649
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:06:49.949 --> 00:06:52.985
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

